#{[17592186499487 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499357 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499487 #edb/ref 1069 "q-87683" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499487 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499487 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/23/2375302a1591f251e33b1d3813e8ea500ce0f7cb85082513d67c30e09e5b8670.png)\n\nGiven the polygons shown above, which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. $C≅V$\n\nII. $V≅B$\n\nIII. $B≅C$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499487 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6b6253c5-dbcb-5eca-bada-e65b2d371ca0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499487 #edb/ref 1074 "In general, congruent polygons have the same shape. In other words:\n\n- they have the same number of sides,\n\n- all corresponding sides are congruent, and\n\n- all corresponding interior angles are congruent.\n\nWith that in mind, let's compare the given polygons.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that the polygons $C$ and $V$ have the same number of sides, but they don't seem to have the same shape. In particular, we notice they don't have corresponding congruent sides.\n\n- Statement II is false. By the same reasoning as statement I, we can see that the polygons $V$ and $B$ are not congruent.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the polygons $B$ and $C$ have the same number of sides and all corresponding sides and angles are congruent, we conclude that $B≅C.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499493 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499351 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499493 #edb/ref 1069 "q-87636" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499493 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499493 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/81/81c7a4f9f0b9ab855b22ad7895f5f0d7b1ae3711563e9691117dfd99f878dccb.png)\n\nGiven the polygon $P$ shown above, which of the following polygons is congruent to $P?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499493 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7ca08efa-e8ff-5fd7-8af8-59ae906150bd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499493 #edb/ref 1074 "In general, congruent polygons have the same shape. In other words:\n\n- they have the same number of sides,\n\n- all corresponding sides are congruent, and\n\n- all corresponding interior angles are congruent.\n\nWith that in mind, let's compare each of the given polygons to $P.$\n\n- The polygon $P$ is a pentagon (it has $5$ sides). So, the polygons $S,$ $T,$ and $U$ can't be congruent to the polygon $P$ because they do not have $5$ sides.\n\n- The polygon $Q$ is *not* congruent to $\\,P$ (even though they both have $5$ sides) because $Q$ and $P$ don't have the same shape. In particular, notice that $P$ has two right angles, while $Q$ doesn't have right angles.\n\nOn the other hand, $P$ and $R$ have the same shape: They have congruent corresponding sides and congruent corresponding angles. This implies that $P≅R.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499533 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499377 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499533 #edb/ref 1069 "q-87760" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499533 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499533 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/7c/7c1663caeea9751ccbaa2544792c9c15331accedc6dd757b44154673f34f8ddc.png)\n\nWhat is a correct congruence statement for the quadrilaterals shown in the diagram above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499533 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c4dcd0bc-d6f0-59ef-aa66-febcad580925" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499533 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that $∠M≅∠O.$ \n\nLet's determine the vertices in the second quadrilateral that correspond to $M.$ First, notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}MN & =DE \\\\ MP & =DG \\\\ ∠M & ≅∠D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n- vertex $M$ corresponds to vertex $D,$\n\n- consequently, vertex $O$ corresponds to vertex $F.$\n\nAs for the other two vertices, notice we have two more pairs of congruent angles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∠N & ≅∠E \\\\ ∠P & ≅∠G.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have two more pairs of congruent sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}OP & =FG \\\\ ON & =FE,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n- vertex $N$ corresponds to $E,$ and\n\n- vertex $P$ corresponds to $G.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that a correct congruence statement is\n\n$$\nMNOP≅DEFG.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499557 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499413 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499557 #edb/ref 1069 "q-87759" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499557 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499557 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/56/56c2afca682997fa2ead3f4962d5de4e347d99164e257737f68ddafea385ae4f.png)\n\nWhat is a correct congruence statement for the quadrilaterals shown in the diagram above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499557 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ef93482b-ea08-53c2-acb8-85ef03af9f1c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499557 #edb/ref 1074 "We have four pairs of congruent angles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∠R & ≅∠F \\\\ ∠S & ≅∠E \\\\ ∠T & ≅∠H \\\\ ∠U & ≅∠G.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have four pairs of congruent sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RS & =FE \\\\ ST & =EH \\\\ TU & =HG \\\\ UR & =GF.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince all corresponding sides and angles are congruent, the two quadrilaterals are congruent. In particular\n\n- vertex $R$ corresponds to vertex $F,$\n\n- vertex $S$ corresponds to vertex $E,$\n\n- vertex $T$ corresponds to vertex $H,$ and\n\n- vertex $U$ corresponds to vertex $G.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that a correct congruence statement is\n\n$$\nRSTU≅FEHG.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
